Z||000018||pontiacs4me||04-14-2001||03:45 AM||||I bought 2 cars at once, 2 days apart, one for the body and one for the engine.   I couldnt find the latch on the first car I bought, I probably searched for 20 minutes. so I asked the guy I bought the second car from, and he told me.||||reg||1
Z||000019||Seanh||04-14-2001||08:11 PM||||It took me about 2 days to find the front hood release. I spent about 30 min. at the gas station looking for that release. It also took me over a year to discover that the sunroof came off and went up front. I always wondered what the wind deflecter was.||||reg||1
Z||000020||stimpy||04-14-2001||11:03 PM||||Luckily my dad had a Fiero before I got mine, so he hipped me to all the locations of compartment opening lever thingies, but I hadto tell him how to take the sunroof out. I was a Fiero owner for 2 years before I found out you can take the keys out of the ignition with the car running. ||||reg||1
Z||000021||fropuff||04-15-2001||12:58 AM||||Originally posted by stimpy:<BR>"I was a Fiero owner for 2 years before I found out you can take the keys out of the ignition with the car running."<P>-I don't know if it's just mine, but mine doesn't do this.  It did on the Cutlass, but the key was really worn.  Maybe it was supposed to if you didn't have trunk release?? <BR><P>------------------<BR>Fuzz busters are for those who are worried about being caught.||||reg||1
Z||000022||fierosound||04-15-2001||01:28 AM||||I had to check my owners manual the first time I had to gas up.  Worse...  on 2 different occasions a mechanic broke the lever thinking it was the rear deck release.  Of course, I didn't know about 'till it was time to refill a few days later...||||reg||10
Z||000023||Cozmo||04-15-2001||08:33 AM||||Ya learn somthin' new every day...<BR>I didn't know the e-brake lever went down (none of my Fieros have a functional one tho)<BR>My keys come out when running on only one car... must be an "option", wonder what the RPO code is?<BR>I found my gas cap release before I needed gas, was poking around the interior of the car after I had just purchased it and saw the little lever and pulled it. Nothing seemed to happen, but when I got out I saw the gas door open! Voila! Here's where gas goes <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR><B>C02M0</B><BR><I>Suncoast Fieros</I><BR>'85 silver 2M6 4spd. Targa <B>"Silver Streak"</B><BR>'86 black auto GT <B>"Blackbird"</B><BR>'87 white auto Coupe <B>"The Duke"</B>||||reg||10
Z||000024||jerpro||04-15-2001||09:10 AM||||It took me about a month to find the gas release lever, I used a credit card to pry it open everyday until there was another fiero in the gas station and the kid told me how to do it. He got a good laugh at me.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>||||reg||1
Z||000025||phreakboy4||04-15-2001||09:27 AM||||Well when me and my girlfriend first bought the car we went right to the gas station after a little drive, and spent about 20 mins trying to find the gas tank release lever, we finally ended up calling the people we got it from and they told me. also we spent about a month of sticking the sunroof in the trunk before someone on here told me that it goes up front. and my keys unfortunatley dont come out<P><BR>84 2M4 coupe(sean and Georgia)||||reg||1
Z||000026||cmc||04-15-2001||02:19 PM||||About the "option" to remove your key with the car running--that's just another wonderful feature of an aging GM ignition key cylinder.  Those things are really bad about not requiring keys to keep running.||||reg||1
Z||000027||Four-Banger||04-17-2001||04:42 PM||||The first time I drove my dad's fiero, I was going to work then running some errands.  Well, the thing is, my dad failed to tell me that the car cannot reach the last 1/4 of the gas in the tank.  So when the gauge reads 1/4 of a tank, it's really empty.  It wouldn't have been so bad, but I ran out of gas near Speedway, Indiana on a day in which it was raining and I was wearing my speech clothes to work.  I couldn't figure out what was wrong with it, so I pried open the gas lid using the keys to the car to put some gas in there after walking a few blocks in the rain and still in my good clothes to pay for a gas container.  When I got home, and my dad asked me why I was wet, he told me the whole story and showed me where the release was and that the car cannot reach the last 1/4 of the tank.  Thanks dad, but it was too little, too late.||||reg||2
Z||000028||DaRkLoRD||04-17-2001||06:35 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Four-Banger:<BR><B>The first time I drove my dad's fiero, I was going to work then running some errands.  Well, the thing is, my dad failed to tell me that the car cannot reach the last 1/4 of the gas in the tank.  So when the gauge reads 1/4 of a tank, it's really empty.  It wouldn't have been so bad, but I ran out of gas near Speedway, Indiana on a day in which it was raining and I was wearing my speech clothes to work.  I couldn't figure out what was wrong with it, so I pried open the gas lid using the keys to the car to put some gas in there after walking a few blocks in the rain and still in my good clothes to pay for a gas container.  When I got home, and my dad asked me why I was wet, he told me the whole story and showed me where the release was and that the car cannot reach the last 1/4 of the tank.  Thanks dad, but it was too little, too late.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>This isn't a problem on all Fieros, right? I've never heard this before<P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||5
Z||000029||Steve Normington||04-17-2001||07:04 PM||||E-brake: I can't how many times people have tried to drive my Fieros with the E-brake on because "It's down, so it must be off." Never mind the beeping and the "Brake" trouble light and the fact that you have to floor it to get it to move?!!?!?!<P>Gas Cap: I spent several frustrating minutes trying to close the stupid cap before I realized that the drivers seat belt had gotten caught under the lever. D'oh!<P>------------------<BR>85 SE 2M6||||reg||1
Z||000030||Cameron||04-17-2001||07:55 PM||||hehe I'm pretty lucky on this one ... when I bought my first Fiero the owner probally had that same problems that I was going to have and explained where every thing goes on and in the car.  So I don't have any funny stories.  * evil laugh * Muhahaha<P><P>------------------<BR>Cameron<BR> <A HREF="http://Fiero.NetMARC.com" TARGET=_blank>http://Fiero.NetMARC.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000031||WKDFIRO||04-18-2001||02:48 AM||||Somehow I knew where the release was for the hood and for the gas cap. I don't remember who told me or how I found out I just seemed to always know. I figure someone must have told me or I just saw it.<P>No one told me and I sure as hell didn't see where the gas release was a stupid fricking Ford Taurus thingy. I wandered around the stupid car for half an hour "checking the tires" and when I had admited defeat, reavowed my hatred and contempt of the F-word car I went into the glove compartment to get the manual out. <P>WHO THE HELL PUTS THE GAS CAP RELEASE IN THE GLOVE COMPARTMENT????<P>What kind of STONED out engineer came up with that twisted idea????||||reg||1
Z||000032||sqoach||04-18-2001||12:49 PM||||Hehe...it took me a little while to find the gas cap release. I found the hood release pretty easy though. I still put my sunroof in the trunk cuz there's no little pad things for me to put it on up front  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>------------------<BR>sqoach@yahoo.com<P>            <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sqoach2.JPG">   <BR>84 SE (project car?)<BR>85 Coupe (parts car)<BR>86 SC (daily driver)||||reg||1
Z||000033||Tigger||04-18-2001||01:03 PM||||Took me a minute to find it for the first time.  Tried looking around E-brake lever and behind the seat thinking "What the.. where is it?   huh?"<P>Short time later I was going somewhere with my Dad and stopped at a station.  He told me "Go run inside and pay, I'll pump."  Without thinking I did.  When I came back a couple minutes later he was still looking for it.  Doh!||||reg||1
Z||000034||Voytek||04-18-2001||02:57 PM||||Posthumane - r'you a member of the Calgary FOG?  If not, check out our web site (meeting this Thursday at Willies on Macleod).||||reg||1
Z||000035||Cliff Pennock||04-18-2001||03:08 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by cmc:<BR><B>About the "option" to remove your key with the car running--that's just another wonderful feature of an aging GM ignition key cylinder.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Actually, those locks are <B>designed</B> to do that. When I bought my first Fiero I found out you could take out the keys while the ignition was in the ON position because I had read it in the user manual.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000036||StuGood||04-18-2001||03:10 PM||||Gas Cap Door Release:  I cheated on this one.  A friend who owned a Fiero before I got mine, gave me a ride in his, and told me all about it.  "The release lever is right behind your left ear."  A year or more later, when I bought my first Fiero, I remembered his words of wisdom.<P>E-Brake:  Shortly after I bought that same (brand new) first Fiero, I took a trip "back home again to Indiana" in it, to visit family.  Parked it in my Dad's garage, with the E-brake set.  <P>One of my brothers (a mechanic) asked if he and his wife could borrow it to take a 15-mile trip to go visit my other brother (and, no, their names aren't Darrell  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">!! ), just to try the car out.  I hesitated , but finally said, "Sure - go ahead."  I mean, he is my brother, and definitely no dummy especially when it comes to automobiles.  What could it hurt?<P>He went out to the garage; I stayed in the house visiting.  A few minutes later, he walked back into the house and asked me, "The parking brake - it's non-functional - ?"  I said, "No.  You lift the lever up, hold the button down, and let the lever down.  It goes down after you pull it, so it won't be in your way getting out of the car."  He said, "Oh - okay, I see."  He looked a little sheepish, went back to the car and off they went. <P>I was a little miffed, because at that point I knew he had moved the car out of the garage with the parking brake dragging!  My brand new car, too!  But (hopefully) I didn't let my irritation show too much.  None the worse for the wear, and at least he was smart enough to stop driving it, and come in to ask!         ||||reg||10
Z||000037||Old Lar||04-18-2001||05:16 PM||||The problem I have is filling up my four door Buick. I get out of the car and reach inside to release the gas cap, which isn't there..Doh!  I do it almost every time.||||reg||3
Z||000038||cmc||04-21-2001||10:02 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Cliff Pennock:<BR><B> Actually, those locks are [b]designed</B> to do that. When I bought my first Fiero I found out you could take out the keys while the ignition was in the ON position because I had read it in the user manual.[/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Huh.  That's interesting.  When I first noticed that in a Camaro (yes, I know guys, boo hiss), I figured it was due to wear, like a heavy set of keys constantly hanging down on it.  There are other issues with that lock related to wear, though.  Problems that could lead to people driving off with your car, so I'm a little wary of just saying them on a public forum.  It's probably something you've noticed, though, nothing incredibly obscure.<p>[This message has been edited by cmc (edited 04-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000039||killerb15||04-21-2001||10:29 PM||||I also could pull the keys out of the ignition in our '86 camaro while it was on.<P>Also, someone PLEASE TELL ME where the gas-cap release is on a fiero, as I plan to buy a fastback this fall, and with all the excitement of my first-car i might forget to ask the previous owner, i never considered this to be a problem, but with all these stories i don't want to have anything 'stupid' to happen to me.<P>Thanks in advance- ||||reg||12
Z||000040||Old Lar||04-21-2001||10:49 PM||||We can't tell a non Fiero owner.  It's like a secret handshake.  But if you scratch your left ear or left side of your head while sitting in a Fiero, you are bound to locate it.||||reg||1
Z||000041||fldevel||04-22-2001||02:17 PM|||| let my dad drive my fiero one day and of course he was considerate and went to put $5 of gas in ..haha the wrath .. he spent about 5 min looking for it ... even went in and asked the person working at the counter in tha gas station ..he even asked a lady that was pumpin gas in the next isle that claimed that she HAD a fiero .. yet she couldent find it either ... so my dad finnly gets frusterated avter about 30 min and decides to come home .. so he walks in the door and hands me the keys and a $10 bill .. and goes .. "i went to put gas in but couldent figure out how to get the damn thing open " ... still pick on him to this day .lol <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000042||Spektyr||04-22-2001||02:58 PM||||Hmm... I wonder what the connection between height and speed of gas cap release discovery is.<P>I found it right off, and I'm 6'4".  What difference does that make?  I barely fit inside the car, and absolutely cannot reach the seatbelt with my left arm.  So I have to twist around to grab the belt with my right arm.  First time I reached for the belt, I turned to see where the belt was and bingo, a funny little lever.   I just have to know what all the buttons do, and this one has a picture of a gas pump on it.  2 + 2 = 4.<P>Rear deck lid:  Found the button right off, swore it was broken.  Pulled up in front of the house, pushed it a dozen times in both directions... nothing.  (The engine was running and the brake was off.)  Figured out that it has to be off, or the brake on when I read the wiring diagram.  (I'm an electronics geek.)<P>Hood release:  Never would've found this... at my height I'd have to be stuck half under the car to see it.  I had to ask the previous owner.<P>Parking Lever: Mine doesn't stay up... I thought it was broken, and apparently it sorta is since it doesn't stay up.  Nice to know it's pretty much the way it was designed.<P>Sun roof storage: figured that out before I knew it came out.  Saw the wind deflector, though, "Oooo... that looks like the deflector on my buddy's aftermarket sunroof."  Pulled the sunroof out of the car and noticed it was about the same width as the front seal... bingo, it fit.<P>Is there an easy trick to making the front of the sunroof seal when you put it back in?  Mine whistles most of the time.<P>------------------<BR>85 GT (to be modified...)<BR>--Um, no the paint isn't oxidized, that's the ultra-rare Whirlpool White textured finish... yeah.<BR>89 Lincoln Town Car (has Fiero Envy)<BR>--Hey!  Can I have an anti-sway bar too?<p>[This message has been edited by Spektyr (edited 04-22-2001).]||||reg||1
